---
title: Soy sauce eggs
servings: 4
yield: 4 eggs
tags: [japanese, make-ahead]
cuisine: japanese
time: 8 hours 15 minutes
source: Ramen Eggs (Ajitsuke Tamago) by Namiko Hirasawa Chen, Just One Cookbook, https://www.justonecookbook.com/ramen-egg/
---

== Marinade ==

Whisk the @soy sauce{1/4%cup}, @mirin{1/4%cup}, @sake{1/4%cup}(or water) and
@sugar{1%tsp} in a #small saucepan{} until the sugar dissolves. Bring to a
boil, simmer ~{1%minute}, then let it cool completely.

== Eggs ==

Bring the @water{4%cups} to a full boil in a #saucepan{}; it should cover the
eggs by an inch.

Lower the @eggs{4}(large, straight from the fridge) in one at a time and cook
~{7%minutes} at a gentle simmer. Roll them gently for the first few minutes to
keep the yolks centred.

Move them straight into iced water for ~{15%minutes}, then peel, starting at
the wide end.

== Marinate ==

Put the eggs and cooled marinade in a #zip-top bag{}, press out the air and
close the bag tight above the eggs so they stay covered. Refrigerate
~{8%hours}, or overnight, but no longer than a day.

Halve them lengthwise to serve. They keep 3 to 4 days in the fridge.

> For runny yolks cook 6 to 6 1/2 minutes; for firmer, custardy ones, 8 to 9.
